# Non-equivalent D-A copolymerization strategy towards highly efficient polymer donor for polymer solar cells

$^1$ Beijing National Laboratory for Molecular Sciences, CAS Key Laboratory of Organic Solids, Institute of Chemistry, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100190, China; 2School of Chemical Science, University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China; 3College of Chemistry, Green Catalysis Center, Zhengzhou University, Zhengzhou 455001, China; 4State Key Laboratory of Advanced Technology for Materials Synthesis and Processing, Wuhan University of Technology, Wuhan 430070, China; 5Foshan Xianhu Laboratory of the Advanced Energy Science and Technology Guangdong Laboratory, Xianhu Hydrogen Valley, Foshan 528216, China; 6Laboratory of Advanced Optoelectronic Materials, College of Chemistry, Chemical Engineering and Materials Science, Soochow University, Suzhou 215123, China
D-A copolymerization is a broadly utilized molecular design strategy to construct high efficiency photovoltaic materials for polymer solar cells (PSCs), and all the D-A copolymer donors reported till now are the alternate D-A copolymers with equal Dand A-units. Here, we first propose a non-equivalent D-A copolymerization strategy with unequal D- and A-units, and develop three novel non-equivalent D-A copolymer donors (PM6-D1, PM6-D2 and PM6-D3 with D/A unit ratio of 1.1:0.9, 1.2:0.8 and 1.3:0.7, respectively) by inserting more D units into the alternate D-A copolymer PM6 backbone to finely tune the physicochemical and photovoltaic properties of the polymers. The three non-equivalent D-A copolymers show the down-shifted highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) energy levels, higher hole mobility, higher degree of molecular self-assembly and higher molecular crystallinity with the increase of D-unit ratio in comparison with the alternate D-A copolymer PM6. As a result, all the three non-equivalent D-A copolymer-based PSCs with Y6 as acceptor achieve improved power conversion efficiency (PCE) with higher $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ , larger $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and higher FF simultaneously. Particularly, the PM6-D1:Y6 based PSC achieved a high PCE of $17.71\%$ , which is significantly higher than that $(15.82\%)$ of the PM6:Y6 based PSC and is one of the highest performances in the binary PSCs.
## polymer solar cells, conjugated polymer donor materials, donor-acceptor copolymers, non-equivalent D-A copolymerization
## 1 Introduction
Donor-acceptor (D-A) copolymerization has proven its effectiveness and has been broadly utilized to construct highperformance organic semiconductor materials for the applications in organic electronic devices, such as organic lightemitting diodes [1–3], polymer solar cells (PSCs) [4–12], and organic field-effect transistors [13–15]. In particular, DA copolymer electron donors are cornerstone of highly efficient PSCs, and their constant innovations have greatly boosted the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of the PSCs [16].
For PSCs, high PCE is the synergistic results of high opencircuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ , large short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ and high fill factor (FF), which mainly depends on the finetuning photoactive molecular structures [17–28]. The molecular backbone of D-A copolymers determines the fundamental properties of the polymers, such as absorption spectra and electronic energy levels [29–32]. In the early stages, molecular design guided by the D-A copolymerization strategy mainly focused on the development of various electron-rich blocks (known as D units) and electron-deficient blocks (known as A units) [33]. By alternately incorporating various D-A couples, the researchers developed numerous highly efficient polymer donors with quite different photovoltaic properties, and thus the photovoltaic performance of the PSCs was drastically promoted [34]. Later, to further improve PCE of the PSCs, strategies such as side-chain optimization (introducing conjugated side-chain and heteroatom alkyl chain) and functional substitutions (such as fluorine substitution and ester substitution) were widely explored and utilized to tune the physicochemical and photovoltaic properties of the polymer donors [35–41].
Throughout the reported high-efficiency polymer donors, almost all the molecular design strategies follow the pattern from design of “monomer units” (including the D-unit, Aunit and $\pi$ -bridge unit) to the “alternate copolymerization”, and the molecular regulation is almost focused on monomer replacement and monomer optimization by side-chain engineering and functionalization. In fact, molecular backbone conformation modulation is of great significance for tuning molecular energy levels, charge carrier transport, packing characteristic and so on, but it has drawn less attention [42]. On the other hand, since the emergence of the D-A copolymerization strategy, all of the copolymers, including the burgeoning random ternary copolymers, were constructed with strictly equal D units and A units [43,44]. Moreover, although the molecular design methods of D-A copolymers are getting mature due to the sophisticated methodologies developed in this field, the underlying mechanisms of the structurally elaborated copolymers are still vague.
Based on all above, here we first proposed a non-equivalent D-A copolymerization strategy and developed three novel non-equivalent D-A copolymer donors (PM6-D1, PM6-D2 and PM6-D3, as shown in Figure 1) by inserting more F-BDT (fluorine substituted bithienyl benzodithiophene) D-units into the alternate D-A copolymer PM6 backbones, which could gain insight into the molecular conformation properties of the D-A copolymers and further improve the PCE of the PSCs. We found that the three copolymers showed gradually down-shifted highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) energy levels with increasing the F-BDT unit proportion, which is beneficial to achieve higher $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ . Moreover, all the three copolymers exhibit higher charge carrier mobility, higher degree of molecular self-assembly and higher molecular crystallinity than the alternate D-A copolymer PM6. Moreover, compared with PM6:Y6 blend active layers, all the three $\mathrm{PM}6{\cdot}\mathrm{D}n$ ( $\scriptstyle\overbrace{n=1}$ , 2, 3):Y6 blend active layers showed more efficient exciton dissociation, less bimolecular recombination and faster charge transportation. As a result, all the PSCs based on PM6-Dn:Y6 achieved higher $V_{\mathrm{oc}},J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and FF simultaneously, and the PM6-D1:Y6 based PSC demonstrated the highest PCE of $17.71\%$ , which is significantly higher than that $(15.82\%)$ of the ${\mathrm{PM}}6{:}\mathrm{Y}6$ based PSC and is one of the highest PCE in the binary PSCs. Since almost all the donor materials applied in PSCs nowadays are based on D-A conjugated structure, this novel and effective non-equivalent D-A copolymerization strategy proposed in this work could provide new molecular design ideas.
## 2 Results and discussion
Synthetic route and molecular structures of the alternate D-A copolymer donor PM6 and the three non-equivalent D-A copolymer donors (PM6-D1, PM6-D2 and PM6-D3) are depicted in Figure 1(a). The detailed synthesis procedures and general characterizations are described in the Supporting Information online. PM6 is composed of 2,6-(4,8-bis(5-(2- ethylhexyl-3-fluoro)thiophen-2-yl)-benzo[1,2-b:4,5-b′]dithiophene) unit and 5,7-bis(2-ethylhexyl)-benzo[1,2-c:4,5- $c^{\prime}]$ dithiophene-4,8-dione unit. The number-average molecular weights $(M_{\mathrm{n}})$ of PM6 and the three copolymers PM6- D1, PM6-D2, PM6-D3 are measured to be 29.9, 35.0, 32.9 and $27.1\mathrm{kDa}$ by high-temperature gel permeation chromatography (GPC), and their corresponding polydispersity index (PDI) are 2.1, 1.8, 1.5 and 1.6, respectively. All these copolymers possess good solubility in common organic solvents.
The simplified molecular orbital theory explanation of the alternate D-A copolymers is shown in Figure 1(b). The electrons of donor unit and acceptor unit re-distribute from their original non-interacting orbitals of the D- and A-units to the new hybridized orbitals of the D-A copolymer, generating higher HOMO and lower LUMO (the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital) energy levels. To better understand the effect of non-equivalent D:A ratio configuration on energy level alignment, electronic energy levels of the four copolymers were measured by electrochemical cyclic voltammetry shown in Figure S2 (Supporting Information online) and the energy diagrams of the copolymers are shown in Figure 1(c). The $E_{\mathrm{HOMO}}$ values of PM6, PM6-D1, PM6-D2, PM6-D3 are $-5.50$ , $-5.51$ , $-5.53$ and $-5.56\mathrm{eV},$ respectively (Figure 1(c) and Table S1). The lower $E_{\mathrm{HOMO}}$ of the polymers with more D units could be due to the strong electron-deficient effect of F-substitution on the D-unit, which is beneficial for obtaining higher $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ in PSCs.
The normalized ultraviolet-visible (UV-Vis) absorption spectra of the copolymers in films, in solutions and the copolymer:Y6 blend films are shown in Figure 1(d) and Figure S3, and the corresponding optical data are listed in Table S1 (Supporting Information online). As shown in Figure 1(d), compared with PM6, the non-equivalent D-A copolymers exhibit slightly blue-shifted maximum absorption peak and weaker shoulder peak with increasing F-BDT unit proportions. The absorption edge of PM6, PM6-D1, PM6-D2, and PM6-D3 films are located at around 668, 667, 677, and $663\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, and their corresponding optical bandgaps are 1.85, 1.86, 1.83, and $1.87\mathrm{eV},$ respectively. As shown in Figure S2, the absorption edge of the $\mathrm{PM}6{\cdot}\mathrm{D}n$ $(n{=}1,2,3){\mathrm{:}}\mathrm{Y}6$ blend films exhibit slight bathochromic shift compared with the PM6:Y6 blend, which is beneficial for extending the solar spectrum coverage and increasing the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of corresponding PSCs.
binary PSCs.
The external quantum efficiency (EQE) of PM6-based and the three non-equivalent D-A copolymers-based PSCs is measured to study the detailed photon conversion process, and to confirm their photovoltaic performance (Figure 2(b)). The results show that all PSCs possess strong photoelectric conversion capability in the wavelength range from 430 to $850\mathrm{nm}$ . Notably, the PM6-D1:Y6 based device displayed the maximum EQE value over $85\%$ and kept above $80\%$ from 450 to $760\mathrm{nm}$ . In comparison with PM6:Y6 based device, the $\mathrm{PM}6{\cdot}\mathrm{D}n$ $_{n=1}$ , 2, 3):Y6 based devices show slightly declined EQE values from 300 to $420\mathrm{nm}$ but enhanced EQE values from 420 to $850\mathrm{nm}$ . The current density values integrated from the EQE curves under the AM 1.5G spectrum are 24.63, 25.66, 25.51, and $25.26\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ for the PSCs based on PM6:Y6, PM6-D1:Y6, PM6-D2:Y6, and PM6-D3:Y6, respectively, which are well consistent with the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values measured from $J_{-}V$ curves within $4\%$ mismatch.
Efficient exciton dissociation and charge carrier transportation are the basis for achieving large $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ . In this work, the dependence of photocurrent density $(J_{\mathrm{ph}})$ on effective voltage $(V_{\mathrm{eff}})$ of the corresponding optimal devices was studied to better understand the effect of non-equivalent D unit and A unit in the three copolymer donors on the exciton dissociation and charge transport behavior (Figure 2(c)). $J_{\mathrm{ph}}$ is defined as $J_{\mathrm{ph}}{=}J_{\mathrm{L}}{-}J_{\mathrm{D}}$ , where $J_{\mathrm{L}}$ and $J_{\mathrm{D}}$ are the current densities under illumination and in the dark, respectively. $V_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is defined as $V_{\mathrm{eff}}{=}V_{0}{-}V_{\mathrm{bias}},$ where $V_{0}$ is the voltage when $J_{\mathrm{ph}}$ is zero and $V_{\mathrm{bias}}$ is the applied voltage bias. $V_{\mathrm{eff}}$ determines the electric field in the blend films and thus affects the exciton dissociation and charge transport. The charge carriers move quickly to the corresponding electrodes and $J_{\mathrm{ph}}$ reaches saturation $(J_{\mathrm{sat}})$ at a high $V_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 1 $\mathrm{\Delta}V_{\mathrm{eff}}{\geq}2\mathrm{\DeltaV})$ , suggesting that all photogenerated charge carriers are extracted and collected with minimal recombination. Therefore, the exciton dissociation probability of PSCs can be judged by calculating the value of $J_{\mathrm{ph}}/J_{\mathrm{sat}}$ . Under short-circuit condition, the calculated $J_{\mathrm{ph}}/J_{\mathrm{sat}}$ values are 0.971, 0.983, 0.982, and 0.982 for the PM6-based and PM6-Dn $(n{=}1,2,3)$ -based PSCs, respectively. And under maximum power output condition, the calculated $J_{\mathrm{ph}}/J_{\mathrm{sat}}$ values are 0.841, 0.879, 0.864, and 0.860 for the PM6-based and PM6-Dn $(n{=}1,2,3)$ -based PSCs, respectively. The results indicate that these three nonequivalent D-A copolymers-based PSCs, especially the PM6-D1:Y6 based PSC, possess faster and more efficient exciton dissociation in comparison with the PM6-based PSC.
In addition to exciton dissociation and charge transportation, charge carrier recombination also greatly affects the value of $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ . To better understand the effect of non-equivalent D unit and A unit in the three copolymer donors on charge carrier recombination mechanism in the corresponding PSCs, the dependence of $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ on the light intensity $(P_{\mathrm{light}})$ in the devices was studied (Figure 2(d)). The relationship between $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and $P_{\mathrm{light}}$ can be described as $J_{\mathrm{sc}}\propto\left(P_{\mathrm{light}}\right)^{\alpha}$ , where the $\ensuremath{a}$ value indicates the degree of bimolecular recombination. If all photogenerated charge carriers are delivered and collected at the corresponding electrodes prior to recombination, the $\scriptstyle a$ value should be 1, while $a{<}1$ indicates some extent of bimolecular recombination. All the four optimal devices show a linear dependence between $\log\ensuremath{J_{\mathrm{sc}}}$ and $\mathrm{log}P_{\mathrm{light}},$ the $\boldsymbol{a}$ values are 0.992, 0.999, 0.981, and 0.997 for the PM6-based and PM6-Dn $(n{=}1,~2,~3)$ -based PSCs, respectively. The $\scriptstyle{a=0.999}$ for the PM6-D1:Y6 based device indicates that there is almost no bimolecular recombination in its active layer. In general, exciton dissociation and charge carrier recombination are directly related to the FF of PSCs. Thus, the most efficient exciton dissociation and the minimal charge carrier recombination in the PM6-D1:Y6 based device are responsible for its highest FF value $(78.7\%)$ .
The charge transport capability of photovoltaic materials and active layers directly affects the FF values of the related PSCs. Firstly, hole mobilities $(\mu_{\mathrm{h}})$ of the four copolymer donors were measured using space-charge-limited current (SCLC) method with the hole-only devices (ITO/PEDOT: PSS/copolymer donor/Au), as shown in Figure S4. All the three non-equivalent D-A copolymers exhibit higher $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}$ than PM6 $(1.05{\times}10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ , especially for PM6-D1 with the highest $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}$ of $1.69{\times}10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{\bar{s}}^{-1}$ . Moreover, the electron mobilities $(\mu_{\mathrm{e}})$ and $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}$ of the four blend active layers were measured using electron-only devices $\mathrm{(ITO/ZnO/co-}$ polymer donor:Y6/PDINO/Al) and hole-only devices (ITO/ PEDOT:PSS/copolymer donor:Y6/Au), respectively. The $\mu_{\mathrm{e}}/{}$ $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}$ values of the PM6:Y6 blend film and the PM6-Dn $(n{=}1,2$ , 3):Y6 films are $1.03\times{10}^{-4}/{1.06}{\times10}^{-4}$ , $1.91{\times}10^{-4}/2.49{\times}10^{-4}$ , $1.03\times{10}^{-4}/{1.89}{\times10}^{-4}$ , and $1.02{\times}10^{-4}/1.14{\times}10^{-4}$ , respectively. Compared to PM6:Y6 based devices, the three non-equivalent D-A copolymers:Y6 based devices exhibit higher $\mu_{\mathrm{e}}$ and $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}$ values. Especially, the PM6-D1:Y6 based device possesses the highest $\mu_{\mathrm{e}}$ of $1.91{\times}10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and $\mu_{\mathrm{h}}$ of 2.49×10−4 cm2 V−1 s−1, which should contribute to higher Jsc and FF.
Molecular stacking characteristics and the related micromorphology of blend films have significant influence on the exciton dissociation process at the donor/acceptor interface as well as charge carrier transport behavior and thus the photovoltaic performance of the PSCs. To further understand the enhanced photovoltaic performance of the three nonequivalent D-A copolymers-based PSCs in comparison with the PM6-based PSC, the grazing incidence wide-angle X-ray scattering (GIWAXS) experiments were performed to probe the molecular packing of the four D-A copolymer donors and their blend films with Y6 acceptor, as shown in Figure 3. From their GIWAXS patterns (Figure 3(a)), all the four D-A copolymers exhibit clear molecular crystalline order and preferential face-on molecular orientation with well-defined $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking diffraction peaks ((010) peak) in the out-ofplane (OOP) direction, which is supposed to contribute to efficient charge transport in the vertical direction of the substrate. For the neat PM6 film, the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking diffraction peak and laminar diffraction peak ((100) peak) located at 1.69 and $0.29\textup{\AA}^{-1}$ in the OOP direction, corresponding to the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking distance of $3.72\mathrm{\AA}$ and lamellar distance of $21.44\mathrm{\AA}$ , respectively. The non-equivalent D-A copolymers PM6-D1 and PM6-D3 films exhibit well defined diffraction peak signals and strong diffraction intensities with a $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking distance of $3.74\mathrm{\AA}$ and lamellar distance of $21.52\mathrm{\AA}$ . The crystal coherence length (CCL) of PM6 and $\mathrm{PM}6{\cdot}\mathrm{D}n$ $(n{=}1,2,3)$ in the OOP direction is calculated to be 27.6, 32.3, 30.8, and $29.8\mathrm{\AA}$ from the Scherrer equation, respectively, indicating that three non-equivalent D-A copolymer donors possess higher degree of molecular self-assembly in the faceon oriented crystallites and higher molecular crystallinity (longer CCL values) than PM6.
The GIWAXS results of the blend films reflect microstructural features with the diffraction patterns, which is composed of individual copolymer donor and acceptor components (Figure 3(b)). Thus, similar to the molecular stacking characteristics of the four neat D-A copolymer films as mentioned above, the PM6:Y6 blend film and $\mathrm{PM}6{\cdot}\mathrm{D}n$ $(n{=}1,\ 2,\ 3){:}\mathrm{Y}6$ blend films exhibit predominant face-on molecular packing orientation with well-defined $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking diffraction peaks in the OOP direction and the lamellar stacking peaks in the in-plane (IP) direction. And with the increase of D unit proportion, the corresponding nonequivalent D-A copolymers:Y6 blend film displays the larger coherence length in the (100) peak along the IP direction, indicating the more orderly molecular self-assembly of the polymer donor. Moreover, the scattering peak located at $q=$ $0.43\textup{\AA}^{-1}$ , which is corresponding to the non-fullerene acceptor Y6, also becomes more pronounced in the nonequivalent D-A copolymers:Y6 blend films. The improved crystallization in both donor polymer and non-fullerene acceptor Y6 has led to more favorable for charge carrier transport, showing a good agreement with the mobility measured from SCLC measurement.
Moreover, the atomic force microscopy (AFM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) measurements were conducted to characterize the four blend films morphologies, and the results are shown in Figure S5. It can be seen that all the four blend films exhibit uniform and clear nano-fiber texture. With the D unit proportion increasing, the corresponding PM6-Dn $(n{=}1,2,3){\mathrm{:}}\mathrm{Y}6$ blend films exhibit bigger fibrous structure. The root-mean square roughness $(R_{\mathrm{q}})$ of the PM6:Y6 blend film and PM6-Dn $\scriptstyle(n=1$ , 2, 3):Y6 blend films is 2.23, 3.17, 3.61 and $3.77\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively.
Moreover, femtosecond transient absorption (fs-TA) spectroscopy measurements are employed on PM6:Y6 and PM6-D1:Y6 blend films to understand the difference in charge transfer dynamics causing by the non-equivalent D-A copolymerization strategy, as shown in Figure 4. Obvious ground state bleach (GSB) signals at the shorter wavelength range are observed from both PM6:Y6 and PM6-D1:Y6 blend films. As the excitation photon energy at $850\mathrm{nm}$ is much smaller than that required for exciton absorption of the copolymers PM6 and PM6-D1, those bleaching signals can be naturally assigned to the hole transfer from acceptor Y6 to copolymer donors. Broad GSB signal with a peak at around $840\mathrm{nm}$ is observed immediately after excitation, corresponding to the GSB and stimulated emission of absorption transition in Y6 due to photoexcitation. GSB signal in the shorter wavelength range (around $636\mathrm{nm}$ for both PM6:Y6 blend film and PM6-D1:Y6 blend film) is observed simultaneously, which should be attributed to the ultrafast hole transfer from Y6 excited state to PM6 or PM6-D1, generating $\mathbf{P}\mathbf{M}6^{+}{-}\mathbf{Y}6^{-}$ or $\mathrm{PM}6{\cdot}\mathrm{D}1^{+}{\cdot}\mathrm{Y}6^{-}$ charge transfer (CT) state. We quantify the hole transfer dynamics with a biexponential decay function, in which the former is assigned to ultrafast exciton dissociation at the PM6 or PM6-D1/Y6 interfaces, and the latter is assigned to exciton diffusion time in Y6 toward interfaces before hole transfer. The hole transfer features rise biexponentially with time constants of 0.42 and 7.4 ps for the PM6:Y6 blend film, and 0.31 and 6.1 ps for the PM6-D1:Y6 blend films, respectively, indicating more faster hole transfer in the PM6-D1:Y6 blend films from Y6 to PM6-D1. The faster charge transfer guarantees a higher yield of the CT state, leading to more efficient photocurrent generation. And with the decay of Y6 anion bleach peak at $860\mathrm{nm}$ , clear bleach peaks at 588 and $637\mathrm{nm}$ appear in the TA spectra. These features decay concomitantly to non-zero over the 6 ns time window, which means that the charge recombination is slow. The long-lived CT state provides enough time for charge migration and collection, which is crucial for high performance PSCs.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, we proposed a non-equivalent D-A copolymerization strategy, and developed three novel non-equivalent D-A copolymer donors (PM6-D1, PM6-D2, and PM6- D3) by inserting more D units into the alternate D-A copolymer PM6 backbones. We found that the three nonequivalent D-A copolymers showed down-shifted HOMO energy levels, higher hole mobility, higher degree of molecular self-assembly, and higher molecular crystallinity in comparison with the alternate D-A copolymer PM6. As a result, PSCs based on all the three non-equivalent D-A copolymers:Y6 achieved higher $V_{\mathrm{oc}},J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and FF simultaneously, and the PM6-D1:Y6 based PSC demonstrated the best PCE of $17.71\%$ , which is significantly higher than that $(15.82\%)$ of the ${\mathrm{PM}}6{:}{\mathrm{Y}}6$ based PSC. This work can not only help us to better understand the impact of backbone modulation on photovoltaic properties of the polymer donors, but also provide new molecular design ideas in efficient D-A copolymerized organic semiconductors for organic electronic devices.
